# [Number of Closed Islands](https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-closed-islands/)

## Approach: DFS (Depth First Search)

We are given a binary grid where `0` represents land and `1` represents water. A closed island is a connected group of land cells that does not touch the boundary of the grid.

### Algorithm
1. Traverse every cell in the grid.
2. Whenever an unvisited land cell (`0`) is found, start a DFS traversal.
3. Mark each visited land cell with `count` to avoid revisiting it.
4. If any cell of the island touches the grid boundary, mark the island as not closed using the `boundary` flag.
5. After exploring the entire island, increment the answer only if `boundary` remains `false`.

## Code (C++)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int count = 1;

    void solve(vector<vector<int>>& grid, bool& boundary, int row, int col,
               vector<pair<int,int>>& dr) {
        if (row < 0 || col < 0 ||
            row >= grid.size() || col >= grid[0].size())
            return;

        if (grid[row][col] != 0) return;

        grid[row][col] = count;

        if (row == 0 || col == 0 ||
            row == grid.size() - 1 ||
            col == grid[0].size() - 1)
            boundary = true;

        for (const auto& it : dr) {
            int r = row + it.first;
            int c = col + it.second;

            if (r < 0 || c < 0 ||
                r >= grid.size() || c >= grid[0].size())
                continue;

            solve(grid, boundary, r, c, dr);
        }
    }

    int closedIsland(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {
        vector<pair<int,int>> dr = {
            {-1,0}, {0,1}, {1,0}, {0,-1}
        };

        int row = grid.size(), col = grid[0].size(), ans = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < row; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < col; j++) {
                if (grid[i][j] == 0) {
                    count++;
                    bool boundary = false;

                    solve(grid, boundary, i, j, dr);

                    if (!boundary) ans++;
                }
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** \(O(m \times n)\) — Each cell is visited at most once.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(m \times n)\) — In the worst case, the recursive DFS call stack can contain up to \(m \times n\) cells.

